    "lawCode": "Amd §408, BC L; amd §205, Lim Lil L",
    "lawSection": "Business Corporation Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S7355A\n\nSPONSOR: KAPLAN\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the business corporation law and the limited liability\ncompany law, in relation to directing the secretary of state to provide\ninformation to certain new businesses\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nWould require when upon receipt of a statement of filing that secretary\nof state shall confirm receipt and include useful information for busi-\nness including but not limited to: contact information for the local\nSBDC, local EAC, business mentor NY, and Empire State Development.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 adds a new paragraph 9 in Section 408 of the business corpo-\nration law to require upon receipt of a filing by the Secretary of\nState, receipt shall be confirmed. Include in this receipt shall be\ninformation including but not limited to contact information for the\nregional NYS Small Business Development Center, regional NYS Entrepren-\neurship Assistance Center, Business Mentor NY, and NYS Empire State\nDevelopment.\n\nSection 2 amends Section 205 of the limited liability corporation law to\nrequire upon receipt of an application to reserve a LLC name, receipt\nshall be confirmed. Include in this receipt shall be information includ-\ning but not limited to contact information for the regional NYS Small\nBusiness Development Center, regional NYS Entrepreneurship Assistance\nCenter, Business Mentor NY, and NYS Empire State Development\n\nSection 3 is the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nNew York has many incredible resources available for business' across\nthe State, however what sometimes is lacking is the business knowing\nsuch resources are available. The great work being done by our SBDCs,\nEACs, and ESDC are only valuable when business knows about them and can\nmake use and access these incredible resources. The goal of this legis-\nlation is simple to put before newly forming businesses a list and\ncontact information for these resources. Providing this information at\nthe first stages of a business is critical, it is during this time that\nmost business needs the most help. Resources are available to help these\nstart-up business, but often during the hectic start of opening a busi-\nness they go overlooked, this legislation aims to  have this information\nsent directly to a new business therefore lessening the chance the great\nprograms that are available go overlooked.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNone.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nMinimal.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect on the sixtieth day.\n",
